INSERT INTO "pages" ("slug","title","type","excerpt","body","author","category","featured_image","tags","meta_title","meta_description","keywords","status","views","published_at","created_at","updated_at") VALUES ('gulfood-mihas-2026-which-shows-matter','Gulfood / MIHAS 2026 calendar: which shows matter for buyers','blog','Gulfood, MIHAS and Istanbul 2026: which halal trade shows matter for buyers, what each is best for, and how to prepare.','For halal buyers, the trade-show year has a rhythm: Dubai opens it, Kuala Lumpur anchors the autumn, and Istanbul closes it with standards politics. With Gulfood''s 31st edition behind us (26–30 January 2026), MIHAS weeks away (23–26 September 2026 at MITEC Kuala Lumpur), and the 11th OIC Halal Expo Istanbul ahead (25–28 November 2026), now is the moment to plan travel, samples and certification paperwork around the shows that actually move sourcing decisions. Our full calendar lives on the [trade shows page](/trade-shows); city-level market context sits in our [UAE market guide](/market-guides/uae) and [Malaysia market guide](/market-guides/malaysia).

## Gulfood: the broad F&B sweep (done for 2026 — mine it anyway)

Gulfood 2026 ran 26–30 January across two venues for the first time — Dubai World Trade Centre and the expanded Dubai Exhibition Centre at Expo City — with the organiser reporting 8,500+ exhibitors from 195 countries. For buyers it remains the widest shelf in the industry: meat and poultry, dairy, beverages, fats and oils, seafood and private label in a single week.

What it is best for: first-pass supplier discovery across the full food and beverage range, price benchmarking ahead of Ramadan-season contracting, and meeting the re-export houses that serve the wider GCC, Africa and Central Asia — the re-export logic our [UAE market guide](/market-guides/uae) describes in detail. Buyer tip: even though the 2026 edition has passed, exhibitor lists stay useful for months. Shortlist now, verify each candidate''s halal certificate scope before requesting samples, and diary the next edition — expected early 2027, but confirm dates with the organiser before booking travel.

## MIHAS: the certified-halal anchor (September — go if you go anywhere)

The 22nd Malaysia International Halal Showcase runs 23–26 September 2026 at MITEC Kuala Lumpur, with organiser MATRADE targeting around US$1 billion in trade across 2,380+ booths and some 40,000 visitors from 80 countries. Unlike general food fairs, MIHAS is halal-native: fourteen clusters spanning food, pharmaceuticals, cosmetics, modest fashion, Islamic finance and logistics, with AI featured as a sector for the first time.

What it is best for: meeting suppliers that already hold recognised halal certification, testing JAKIM-recognised scope face to face — background in our [Malaysia market guide](/market-guides/malaysia) and the [JAKIM certifier profile](/certifying-bodies/jakim) — and using the hosted-buyer matchmaking programme for pre-scheduled one-to-ones. Register early: meeting slots at recent MIHAS editions filled well before opening day. Buyer tip: bring your certificate-scope checklist (species, slaughter method, logo-use rights) and ask for it on the stand, not by email afterwards.

## Halal Expo Istanbul: standards, private label and OIC access (November)

The 11th OIC Halal Expo runs 25–28 November 2026 at the Istanbul Expo Center, alongside the World Halal Summit and organised with SMIIC under OIC auspices; the 2025 edition drew around 500 exhibitors and 50,000+ visitors. Announced 2026 themes include natural, organic and vegan lines plus private label — confirm the final programme before travelling, as themes shift year to year.

What it is best for: reading where OIC and SMIIC mutual recognition is heading, meeting Turkish and Central Asian processors, and opening private-label conversations ahead of next year''s Ramadan cycle. Buyer tip: pair the expo floor with the summit sessions if certification strategy — not just SKUs — is on your agenda.

## Two secondaries worth the trip if they fit your route

The Saudi International Halal Expo in Riyadh (early October) is the direct door into the GCC''s largest consumer market and its strict SFDA registration regime. MIHAS @ Dubai (mid-November, Dubai World Trade Centre) puts Malaysian suppliers in front of Gulf buyers without the Kuala Lumpur flight. Both are listed with dates and venues on our [trade shows page](/trade-shows) — treat them as add-ons to the three anchors, not substitutes.

## How to prepare: the buyer''s pre-show file

Shows reward buyers who arrive with paperwork, not just business cards: current supplier halal certificates with scope pages, your target market''s labelling requirements, sample test reports for food-contact packaging where relevant, and a short RFQ template stating volumes and Incoterms. Confirm hosted-buyer deadlines, visa lead times, and halal-certificate recognition between the show country and your import market — recognition gaps, not prices, kill most post-show deals.','HalalNeo Editorial','blog','/api/media/blog-2.webp','["trade-shows","sourcing"]','Gulfood / MIHAS 2026 Calendar: Which Shows Matter for Buyers','Buyer-first calendar for Gulfood, MIHAS and Halal Expo Istanbul 2026: dates, what each show is best for, and pre-show preparation.',NULL,'published',0,1788900000,1788900000,1788900000);

INSERT INTO "market_guides" ("slug","country","flag","region","muslim_population","total_population","market_size_usd","mandate_status","mandatory_since","certifying_bodies","import_requirements","standard_basis","certificate_validity","estimated_cost_usd","processing_time","key_insights","opportunities","challenges","summary","meta_title","meta_description","keywords","status","created_at","updated_at") VALUES ('singapore','Singapore','🇸🇬','Southeast Asia','~600,000+ (about 15% of residents)','~6.1 million (residents ~4.2 million)','$1B+ (domestic halal industry, 2021 est.; re-export hub flows far larger)','voluntary',NULL,'[{"name":"MUIS","slug":"muis"}]','["No blanket halal import mandate — halal certification is voluntary, but any halal claim must be backed by a valid MUIS certificate","MUIS holds sole legal power to issue halal certificates in Singapore under the Administration of Muslim Law Act (AMLA)","Food imports need an SFA-registered importer and SFA import permits; foreign halal certificates should come from MUIS-recognised bodies (FHCB)","Seven MUIS schemes cover eating establishments, endorsements for imported or re-exported products, food preparation areas, poultry abattoirs, whole plants, and storage","English labelling per Singapore Food Agency rules; MUIS mark use follows MUIS logo-use requirements"]','SS 812:2020 (Singapore Standard for Halal Food); Singapore Muis Halal Quality Management System (HalMQ, 10 principles); AMLA sole-authority framework','1–3 years (scheme-dependent; confirm with MUIS)','Varies — roughly SGD 1,000+ per eatery outlet including training and audits; confirm the MUIS fee schedule before budgeting','Typically several weeks to a few months depending on documentation readiness (confirm with MUIS)','["Sole statutory authority: MUIS is the only body that can legally certify halal in Singapore — no competing domestic certifiers","Voluntary but commercially powerful: 4,000+ eateries certified out of 23,600+ retail food establishments, growing about 10% a year (MUIS via Straits Times, 2026)","Enforcement has teeth: only about 75% of new and renewal applications were approved in 2025 — documentation quality decides outcomes","Re-export hub logic: Singapore''s port, cold chain and 27 free-trade agreements make MUIS certification valuable for goods transiting to OIC markets","MUIS certification is recognised in GCC markets including the UAE and Saudi Arabia, per industry certification guides — confirm scope market by market"]','["Premium import demand: high-income, import-dependent food market (agri-food imports around USD 19B in 2025, per USDA) with affluent Muslim and halal-adjacent consumers","Re-export platform: certify or process in Singapore and ride its logistics into Malaysia, Indonesia and the Gulf","Foodservice gap: halal-certified concepts from Korean BBQ to omakase are expanding fast — ingredients and franchise supply follow","Islamic finance and halal pharma/cosmetics adjacency: halal food is the entry wedge into a diversified halal economy"]','["Voluntary regime means no mandate tailwind — every sale must be won on consumer trust and buyer requirements, not compliance deadlines","Small domestic base: ~600,000+ Muslims limits local volume; economics depend on premium pricing and re-export","High operating costs: halal procurement premiums (e.g. ~30% on certified wagyu, per industry reporting) compress foodservice margins","Staffing rules bite: MUIS schemes require minimum Muslim headcount per premises plus Halal Competency Assessment — plan hiring early"]','Singapore is a high-income, voluntary-certification market where MUIS — the sole statutory certifier — underwrites premium domestic demand. Its port, cold chain and trade agreements make it a re-export platform for halal goods into Southeast Asia and the Gulf.',NULL,NULL,NULL,'published',1788900000,1788900000);

INSERT INTO "knowledge_base" ("slug","section","title","summary","body","tags","author","status","views","created_at","updated_at") VALUES (
'primary-vs-secondary-halal-packaging-rules',
'packaging-labeling',
'Primary vs Secondary Halal Packaging Rules: What Applies at Each Layer',
'Which halal rules apply to food-contact, secondary and tertiary packaging: segregation, inks, recycled content and audit focus areas.',
'Halal audits do not stop at the ingredient list — they follow the product through every layer that touches it, stores it or ships it. Primary packaging contacts the food directly; secondary packaging groups retail units; tertiary packaging moves pallets. The halal risk falls steeply from the inner layer outward, but auditors still check each one, and for different reasons. This guide maps what applies where. For the market context behind Gulf enforcement, see our [Malaysia market guide](/market-guides/malaysia).

## The three layers, defined

- **Primary (food-contact) packaging** — films, bottles, cans, trays, closures and liners that touch the food. Highest halal scrutiny: every material, ink, adhesive and processing aid is in scope.
- **Secondary packaging** — cartons, sleeves, shrink wrap and labels that group retail units but do not touch the food. Scrutiny focuses on segregation, identification and label claims rather than chemistry.
- **Tertiary packaging** — pallets, stretch film, crates and containers for transport and warehousing. Scrutiny is almost entirely about segregation and contamination prevention during storage and handling.

## What halal rules apply at the primary layer

Anything that contacts food must itself be halal-compliant: no porcine-derived additives (for example certain stearates, glycerine or gelatin-based coatings), no alcohol-carried coatings that survive into the finished pack, and no lubricants or release agents of animal origin unless their halal status is documented. Suppliers should provide declarations of compliance plus, for high-risk inputs such as gelatin capsules, casings and Montag coatings, their own halal certificates. Where the destination market requires migration evidence as well, read our companion guide on migration testing before the audit — chemical safety files and halal files increasingly get reviewed in the same visit.

## Inks, adhesives and processing aids: the hidden layer

Printing inks, laminating adhesives, varnishes and anti-static or anti-fog coatings sit on or near food-contact surfaces, and auditors ask about them precisely because buyers forget them. Typical expectations: UV-cure and solvent-based inks fully cured per the manufacturer''s specification so no uncured residues transfer; adhesives declared free of animal-derived components or backed by halal documentation; and no alcohol-based cleaning residues on food-contact surfaces after sanitation. Keep technical data sheets for every ink and adhesive on file — a missing data sheet draws more auditor attention than a compliant-but-unfamiliar chemistry.

## Recycled content: halal questions buyers rarely ask

Recycled board, rPET and post-consumer resins are welcome under sustainability rules but raise two halal-adjacent flags. First, provenance: recycled feedstock of unknown origin can carry residues (fats, flavourings, non-halal food traces) that virgin material would not. Second, decontamination evidence: auditors and food-safety reviewers may ask for the super-clean process documentation or challenge-test reports behind food-grade recycled claims. None of this bans recycled content — but using it without a documented chain of custody turns a sustainability win into an audit finding. Confirm the destination market''s current recycled-content position with your certifier, since rules here are still evolving.

## Segregation in storage and handling

From the secondary layer outward, the halal question is physical, not chemical: are halal finished goods, halal packaging materials and non-halal items stored, staged and loaded so they cannot mix? Auditors look for labelled halal zones or racking, pallet wrapping that survives the warehouse, dispatch checks that match certificate scope to the loaded SKUs, and cleaning records for shared conveyors, hoppers and filling heads between runs. Transport counts too — one open-topped pallet of halal cartons under a leaking non-halal load is a finding, not bad luck.

## What auditors actually check: a pre-audit pass

- Map every packaging SKU to its layer and list all inks, adhesives, coatings and aids per SKU.
- File supplier declarations for all food-contact materials; collect halal certificates for high-risk inputs.
- Verify label claims and logo use against live certificate scope, site and dates before printing.
- Confirm recycled-content chain of custody and any food-grade challenge documentation.
- Walk the warehouse: halal zoning, pallet identification, dispatch matching, shared-equipment cleaning logs.
- Diary certificate renewals for both the product and any licensed marks on pack — an expired certificate invalidates every layer at once.',
'["packaging","labelling","food-contact"]','HalalNeo Editorial','published',0,1788900000,1788900000
);

INSERT INTO "knowledge_base" ("slug","section","title","summary","body","tags","author","status","views","created_at","updated_at") VALUES (
'migration-testing-food-contact-packaging',
'packaging-labeling',
'Migration Testing for Food-Contact Packaging: Overall vs Specific Limits',
'What migration testing is, when GCC and EU import rules require it, overall vs specific limits, common failures and how to read a report.',
'Migration testing measures how much chemistry moves from packaging into food. Gulf import controls increasingly ask for it alongside halal certificates, and EU rules set the template most Gulf standards mirror. This guide explains what the tests prove, when they are required, and how to commission and read one. For Gulf market context see our [UAE market guide](/market-guides/uae) and the [MOIAT certifier profile](/certifying-bodies/moiat).

## What migration testing actually measures

A finished pack is a chemical system: monomers, additives, plasticisers, inks, adhesives and coatings can all transfer — migrate — into food, especially with heat, fat content and long storage. Testing exposes the material to food simulants (standardised liquids standing in for watery, acidic, alcoholic and fatty foods) under worst-foreseeable time and temperature conditions, then measures what moved. Two different limits apply to the results, and confusing them is the most common buyer error.

## Overall vs specific migration: the two numbers that matter

- **Overall migration limit (OML)** — the cap on everything that migrates, taken together. Under EU Regulation 10/2011 the OML is 10 mg per dm2 of food-contact surface (or 60 mg per kg of food, the expression used for infant food contact materials). Gulf standard GSO 1863/2013 mirrors this: below 10 mg/dm2 or 60 mg/kg.
- **Specific migration limits (SMLs)** — per-substance caps for individually listed chemicals, expressed in mg per kg of food. Where the EU positive list sets no SML for a substance, a generic 60 mg/kg cap applies. Gulf rules track the EU positive-list structure closely.
- Passing OML but failing one SML still means failure — both gates must be cleared on the same test programme.

## When GCC and EU import rules require it

In the EU, plastic food-contact materials must comply with Regulation 10/2011 as a condition of market access, with declarations of compliance flowing down the supply chain. In the Gulf, GSO 2231/2012 sets the general food-contact safety frame and GSO 1863/2013 carries the plastics detail; Saudi Arabia enforces these mandatorily through SFDA, whose import-control arm samples consignments at port — documentation checks, identity checks and laboratory testing — with non-compliant goods re-exported or destroyed. Exporters should therefore treat migration evidence as part of the standard Gulf technical file (alongside the halal certificate and label approvals), not as an optional extra. Confirm the current edition and national adoptions with your lab, since Gulf states adopt GSO texts with local variations.

## Common failures: what actually trips test reports

- **Plasticisers in flexible films and closures** — phthalate and alternative plasticiser SMLs are frequent failures where film formulations were chosen for cost, not compliance.
- **Printing inks and adhesives** — set-off transfer (ink from the outer printed face migrating through the reel or stack onto the food-contact face) is a classic surprise failure.
- **Recycled board and plastics** — mineral-oil residues and legacy additives from unknown feedstock; recycled content without challenge-test backing fails disproportionately.
- **Heavy metals and residual monomers** — Gulf container standards cap both migration (typically 60 mg/kg overall) and specific elements such as lead, arsenic and chromium; vinyl chloride, styrene and acrylonitrile residues are controlled in the plastics standard.
- **Wrong simulant or condition** — testing a fatty-food pack only in water simulant, or at ambient temperature for a hot-fill application, produces a passing report that proves nothing.

## How to commission a test: lab, simulants, timelines

Use an ISO 17025-accredited laboratory with a food-contact scope covering the destination regulation — ask explicitly whether they test to GSO 1863/2013 as well as EU 10/2011, since simulant assignment and editions differ in detail. Supply the lab with the finished article (not just resin pellets), the food type, the worst-case contact time and temperature, and the destination market list. Typical timelines run two to six weeks depending on whether long-term storage conditions must be simulated — confirm the schedule before you promise ship dates, and budget for retesting if the first formulation fails.

## Reading a test report: five checks before you file it

- Both OML and every applicable SML are reported against named limits — not just a blanket pass statement.
- Simulants and test conditions match your real use (fatty-food simulant for fatty foods; hot-fill or oven conditions where relevant).
- The tested sample is identified as the finished article with batch or lot traceability.
- The report cites the regulation edition tested against (for example EU 10/2011 as amended, GSO 1863/2013).
- Declarations of compliance from upstream suppliers are attached, so the chain from resin to finished pack is documented. Keep the report with the halal certificate and label file: Gulf reviewers increasingly expect the full set together.',
'["packaging","labelling","testing","food-contact"]','HalalNeo Editorial','published',0,1788900000,1788900000
);

